{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "KRIA Docker Execution Guard",
  "description": "Protect KRIA docker_exec execution from host/container confusion and substrate routing errors. Enforce strict distinction between host and container execution contexts, prevent VM routing confusion, prefer local docker inspection when appropriate, and preserve substrate-aware execution aligned with KRIA authoritative orchestration principles.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "preToolUse",
    "toolTypes": [
      "docker_exec"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "You are about to execute a docker_exec tool call in KRIA.\n\nBefore proceeding, enforce docker execution discipline and prevent substrate confusion:\n\nVALIDATE EXECUTION CONTEXT:\n1. Identify target execution context explicitly:\n   - HOST: Direct host system execution (docker CLI, system commands)\n   - CONTAINER: Inside running container (application code, container processes)\n   - DOCKER_DAEMON: Docker daemon operations (image management, network, volumes)\n\n2. Verify context matches operation intent:\n   - Container inspection (docker ps, docker inspect) → HOST (docker CLI)\n   - Container execution (docker exec) → HOST (docker CLI) with container ID\n   - Application execution inside container → CONTAINER (inside running process)\n   - Image operations (docker build, docker pull) → HOST (docker CLI)\n   - Daemon operations (docker network, docker volume) → HOST (docker CLI)\n\nPRESERVE HOST/CONTAINER DISTINCTION:\n- Do NOT execute host commands inside container\n- Do NOT execute container-specific commands on host\n- Do NOT assume container has host tools (curl, git, python, etc.)\n- Do NOT route host filesystem operations to container\n- Do NOT route container process operations to host\n\nDISTINGUISH EXECUTION SCOPES:\n- HOST execution:\n  * Docker CLI commands (docker ps, docker exec, docker build)\n  * Host filesystem access\n  * Host system services and tools\n  * Docker daemon interaction\n  * Network operations from host perspective\n- CONTAINER execution:\n  * Application code and processes\n  * Container filesystem (/app, /home, /tmp within container)\n  * Container environment variables and runtime\n  * Container-specific tools and libraries\n  * Network operations from container perspective\n\nAVOID VM ROUTING CONFUSION:\n- Do NOT route docker_exec to VM substrate\n- Do NOT assume docker daemon exists on VM without verification\n- Do NOT confuse container isolation with VM isolation\n- Do NOT route container operations through VM hypervisor\n- Distinguish:\n  * Docker on HOST: Direct docker daemon access\n  * Docker on VM: Requires VM substrate routing (separate concern)\n  * Container inside VM: Nested isolation (rare, requires explicit handling)\n\nPREFER LOCAL DOCKER INSPECTION:\n- Before executing commands in container, inspect container state:\n  * docker ps (list running containers)\n  * docker inspect (container metadata, environment, mounts)\n  * docker logs (container output and errors)\n  * docker exec --help (verify docker exec availability)\n- Use inspection to:\n  * Verify container is running before docker exec\n  * Verify required tools exist in container\n  * Verify environment variables and mounts\n  * Avoid blind execution in unknown container state\n- Inspection is lightweight and deterministic:\n  * Same container ID → same inspection results\n  * No side effects from inspection\n  * Enables informed execution decisions\n\nVALIDATE DOCKER EXEC PRECONDITIONS:\n- Container ID or name is specified and valid\n- Container is running (not stopped, paused, or exited)\n- Command to execute is appropriate for container runtime\n- Required tools/interpreters exist in container (if applicable)\n- Container has necessary permissions for operation\n- No conflicting operations on same container\n\nPRESERVE SUBSTRATE-AWARE EXECUTION:\n- Docker is execution substrate, not orchestration authority\n- KRIA maintains routing decisions (host vs container)\n- Substrate cannot override execution context\n- Preserve bounded cognition: validate context once, route decisively\n- No implicit substrate fallback chains (HOST → CONTAINER → VM)\n\nPRESERVE BOUNDED ORCHESTRATION:\n- Execution context is determined by operation intent, not command syntax\n- No inference of context from command content\n- Explicit context specification required for ambiguous operations\n- Single execution path per operation (no retry chains)\n- Deterministic routing: same inputs → same execution context\n\nDETECT DANGEROUS PATTERNS:\n- Host operations routed to container (e.g., modifying /etc inside container)\n- Container operations routed to host (e.g., running app code on host)\n- Blind docker exec without container state verification\n- Assuming host tools exist in container\n- Routing docker operations through VM without explicit intent\n- Cross-substrate assumptions (container filesystem as host filesystem)\n- Executing privileged operations in unprivileged container\n\nEXECUTION FLOW:\n1. Identify operation intent (inspect, execute, manage)\n2. Determine target context (HOST, CONTAINER, DOCKER_DAEMON)\n3. If CONTAINER execution:\n   - Inspect container state (docker ps, docker inspect)\n   - Verify container is running and has required tools\n   - Verify command is appropriate for container runtime\n4. If HOST execution:\n   - Verify docker CLI is available\n   - Verify container ID/name is valid\n   - Verify docker daemon is accessible\n5. Route to appropriate substrate (HOST docker CLI or CONTAINER process)\n6. Execute with validated context\n\nIf operation context is explicit and validated, proceed with docker_exec immediately.\n\nIf operation context is ambiguous, missing, or mismatched with operation intent, briefly state the context confusion and require explicit execution context specification before proceeding."
  }
}